SPECIAL ISSUE PAPER 307

Vehicle dynamics modelling for the National Advanced Driving Simulator

G J Heydinger1*, M K Salaani, W R Garrott3 and P A Grygier3 1S.E.A. Inc., Columbus, Ohio, USA 2Transportation Research Center Inc., East Liberty, Ohio, USA 3National Highway TraYc Safety Administration, East Liberty, Ohio, USA

Abstract: This paper provides an overview of the development of a vehicle dynamics simulation model for use on the National Highway TraYc Safety Administration’s (NHTSA’s) National Advanced Driving Simulator. The paper describes fundamental aspects of models used to represent rigid body chassis and suspension systems, powertrain, tyres, brakes, steering and aerodynamics. Representative data from laboratory measurements, instrumented eld tests and simulation runs of a 1997 Jeep Cherokee Sport are presented to illustrate simulation development and validation eVorts. A companion paper to this one also uses Jeep Cherokee data. Both papers highlight current capabilities and methodologies employed by two organizations that have worked, often collaboratively, to advance the state of the art of vehicle dynamics modelling and simulation validation. This paper features work done by NHTSA’s Vehicle Research and Test Center; the companion paper reports on work done by Systems Technology, Incorporated (STI ).
Keywords: National Advanced Driving Simulator, simulator, simulation, vehicle dynamics, model, validation

1 INTRODUCTION
The National Highway TraYc Safety Administration (NHTSA) has funded development of the National Advanced Driving Simulator (NADS ), a driver-in-theloop, high delity and real-time driving simulator. The NADS, shown in Fig. 1, is the most advanced driving simulator in the world and it has been designed to immerse the test subject (driver) fully into the driving scenario. The NADS provides a safe, eYcient tool for driver impairment studies, for driver workload and adaptation studies, and it can be used to support development of automotive safety regulations [1].
Four vehicles have been modelled and are currently available for use on NADS: a Ford Taurus, a Chevrolet Malibu, a Jeep Cherokee and a tractor–semitrailer. One of the vehicles is mounted in the NADS dome. For the light vehicles, the entire vehicle is placed in the dome; for the heavy truck just the tractor cab plus front hood is used. The dome contains a visual system that uses 15 projectors to provide the test subject with a 360° eld of
