The vehicle dynamics simulation computes the response of the vehicle as a result of the test subject (driver) inputs for steering wheel motion, throttle displacement, brake pedal displacement, clutch engagement and transmission gear position. Each of the NADS vehicles is tted with the NADS Control Feel System (CFS) hardware that provides driver feedback through the driver controls: steering wheel, throttle, brakes, clutch and gearshift mechanisms. The terrain features and pro les are modelled as inputs to the vehicle dynamics simulation, In addition, the vehicle dynamics simulation computes the aerodynamic forces arising from simulated steady and gusting winds and those caused by passing vehicles.
The responses computed by the vehicle dynamics simulation include the position, velocity and acceleration states of the vehicle. The visual system image generators that project the real-time images of the driving scenario use these variables. These variables are also used to determine the position of the vehicle on the terrain. They are also used to drive the NADS motion system, which moves the dome to replicate the motions of an actual vehicle driving through the simulated scenario.
The NADS motion system has nine degrees of freedom as indicated on Fig. 1. It has a 330° range of motion yaw ring between its six-degree-of-freedom hexapod and the dome containing the vehicle. The hexapod is mounted on a 20 m ×20 m X–Y motion system that provides for large horizontal excursions and two additional degrees of freedom.
To simulate high frequency road disturbances and high frequency loads through the tyres and suspension, NADS contains four vibration actuators, mounted at points of suspension–chassis interaction. These vibration actuators are mounted between the oor of the dome and vehicle, and they act only in the bounce direction of the chassis. The vibration actuators were designed with vibration isolator counterweights to minimize vibrations transmitted to the dome. The vibration actuators can be driven on the basis of forces computed from the vehicle dynamics simulation and/or on the basis of random forces to mimic road disturbances.
Realistic mathematical vehicle dynamics models are a prerequisite for successful NADS operation. This paper highlights eVorts made by NHTSA’s Vehicle Research and Test Center ( VRTC ) to provide such models. In addition to describing general aspects of the NADS vehicle dynamics simulation, the paper focuses on required laboratory measurements to obtain meaningful parametric values for vehicle models, instrumented eld testing for full model evaluation and simulation model validation. The overall methodology presented is a culmination of over a decade of eVort by VRTC to re ne and streamline simulation development and evaluation.
