2 NADS VEHICLE DYNAMICS
A general purpose, multibody, dynamics program, Real Time Recursive Dynamics ( RTRD), developed by the University of Iowa’s Center for Computer-Aided Design, is the core dynamics code of NADS vehicle dynamics simulation, NADSdyna [9]. NADSdyna includes vehicle subsystems that interact with the RTRD. Six vehicle submodels are included in NADSdyna: powertrain, cruise control, tyres, brakes, steering and aerodynamics. These submodules provide NADSdyna with a broad range of functional capabilities and allow most production vehicles to be simulated. In the NADS application, the inputs to NADSdyna come from the test subject (driver) inputs, from the modelled terrain features and pro le and from the modelled wind. The outputs from NADSdyna at each simulation time step are used to drive the vehicle through the scenario and to direct the motion, visual and audio systems to respond accordingly.
The RTRD is used to model the rigid body behaviour of chassis, wheels and suspension and steering linkages. It oVers great capabilities in terms of rigid body mechanism modelling and accounts for non-linear kinematic and dynamic eVects, such as roll steer, roll centres and squat/lift forces. The RTRD is based on a topological analysis of a rigid body mechanism formulated using minimum relative joint coordinates. It can model either open or closed loop chains. Its constraint library supports revolute, spherical, translational, universal, spherical–translational, revolute–spherical and translational– revolute joints. For closed chains, distance constraints, spherical, and revolute–spherical cut-joint types are supported. Springs, dampers, antiroll bars and leaf springs are modelled as external forces. Non-linear elements can be used.
Two fundamental requirements of a vehicle dynamics simulation used with a driving simulator are that it executes faster than real time and that it provides suYcient delity to the actual vehicle to convince a simulator driver that they are driving the actual vehicle. These two requirements are con icting in that higher simulation delity requires greater model complexity and longer run times. NADSdyna has been developed to meet both of these requirements. NADSdyna has been shown to provide simulated responses that capture the essence of vehicle behaviour necessary for realistic simu-
