NADSdyna does not include models for bushing compliance. Instead, suspension compliance characteristics are added to suspension kinematics, as is typical of lumped parameter based simulation models. NADSdyna also does not include models for non-rigid bodies (e.g. the chassis is modelled as a rigid body). These modelling decisions were made so as not to lengthen simulation run times and not to make NADSdyna more complex than necessary for successful NADS operation.
The exemplar vehicle modelled and tested is a 1997 Jeep Cherokee Sport. It has a 4.0 l I6 engine, four-speed automatic transmission, cruise control, pitman arm power-assisted steering, four-wheel antilock brakes and P255/75R15 Goodyear Wrangler RT/S OWL tyres with cold in ation pressure of 36 lbf/in2.

2.1 Multibody dynamics modelling
A thorough exposure to the multibody dynamics formalisms used by the RTRD goes beyond the scope of this document. Interested parties should refer to specialized references. The RTRD has a recursive formulation that requires rigid bodies to be connected in an open loop structure, or a spanning tree [10, 11]. The Jeep model contains six closed loops. A joint on each loop is cut to obtain a spanning tree and constraint equations associated with Lagrange multipliers are imposed at these cut joints to force kinematically admissible motion. The procedure cuts joints that will result in a model with optimum computational eYciency characterized by a minimum number of coordinates and constraint equations [12]. The overall RTRD model for the Jeep Cherokee is shown in Fig. 2. Table 1 summarizes the rigid bodies and joints modelled.
VRTC subcontracted with SEA, Incorporated, to measure suspension kinematics and compliances, shock absorber characteristics, vehicle and suspension component geometry and centre-of-gravity and inertial properties of the vehicle and suspension components [13]. SEA, Incorporated, used its Vehicle Inertia Measurement Facility to determine vehicle weight, centre-of-gravity longitudinal, lateral and height positions, vehicle roll mass of inertia, pitch mass of inertia, yaw mass of inertia and roll/yaw mass product of inertia. The suspension linkages’ inertial properties were measured using bi lar and tri lar pendulums. The suspension kinematic and compliance measurements were made by SEA using in-house equipment. The shock absorbers were tested at Detroit Testing Labs.
