2.4.4 Tyre model
NADSdyna supports the System Technology, Incorporated (STI ) tyre model [18], a look-up table tyre model and a user-de ned interface tyre model. Using the user-de ned interface subroutine, the user can implement any desired tyre model (e.g. Pajecka Magic Formula Model ). The STI tyre model includes pertinent quasi-static tyre dynamics as well as lateral and longitudinal lag dynamics. Comparisons of tyre model predictions with test data are provided in the companion paper [19]. The tests performed on the Cherokee’s P225/75R15 tyres to obtain data for the model included steering, cambering, braking/driving, combined and discrete sinusoidal (to measure tyre lag characteristics) [4].
2.4.5 Aerodynamic forces and moments
The SAE J1594 aerodynamic recommended practice is used to model aerodynamic forces [20]. The mathematical application is similar to reference [16 ]. NADSdyna also contains a model for wind gusts resulting from passing vehicles. The wind gust model is based on work by HeZey [21].
Aerodynamic force and moment coeYcients for the 1997 Jeep Cherokee were measured at Lockheed Aeronautical Systems Company’s Low Speed Wind Tunnel [22]. The vehicle was installed in the low speed test section on an external balance, four-point, support system. Data were acquired at nominal velocities of 45, 65 and 90 miles/h and at yaw angles spanning from 0° to 180° at 10° increments. The coeYcients shown in Fig. 7 as a function of aerodynamic (wind ) heading angle, â, include lift coeYcient (CL), drag coeYcient (CD), side

force coeYcient (CS ), pitch moment coeYcient (CPM ), yaw moment coeYcient (CYM ) and roll moment coeYcient (CRM ).
2.4.6 Brake system
Power-assisted front disc and rear drum brakes are standard equipment for the Cherokee. The 1997 Cherokee is equipped with a xed rear pressure-proportioning valve. Brake system dynamics is modelled by including a rst-order lag between brake pedal force and pressure at the brake lines. See reference [4] for the mathematical formulation used.
Measurements of brake pedal force, front line brake pressure and rear line brake pressure were done at VRTC. The vehicle was stationary, with engine running, as a cyclic increasing brake pedal force was slowly applied. The brake pedal force to front line brake pressure relation was obtained, and linear regression was used to determine curve data points used in NADSdyna. Rear brake pressure line to front brake pressure line proportioning was similarly determined. The knee point and slope (rear/front) were determined using linear regression at increasing pressures. Hysteresis was not considered for rear-to-front pressure proportioning data. The brake line pressure to brake torque relation is measured using VRTC’s Road Transducer Plates. Brake torques were determined from measured tyre forces multiplied by wheel rolling radii. Linear regression was used to determine curve data points for front and rear brakes used in NADSdyna. The time delay between brake force and front brake line pressure is estimated from a brake force step input test to be 0.2 s.
The ABS model uses a proportional plus derivative controller to drive wheel slip control to a set point. Modulated pressure is computed individually for each wheel so that computations can proceed in parallel with those for wheel brake torque. Basic ABS controller formulations are explained in reference [3 ].
2.4.7 Steering system
On NADS, actual, measured steering wheel angles and rates are fed in real time as inputs to NADSdyna. NADSdyna computes vehicle responses and resulting forces acting on the steering ‘rack’. For the Cherokee, ‘rack’ force is the force at the lower end of the pitman arm. Power steering eVects are handled by NADS CFS software and hardware. CFS models steering feedback on the basis of steering forces and motions and includes a model for on-centre steering lash.

