3.1 Vehicle eld testing
VRTC has done extensive vehicle testing to provide data for NADSdyna evaluation. The Cherokee instrumentation and eld testing are similar to those used to test the Ford Taurus [24]. Vehicle eld testing minimized eVects of driver variability and provided repeatable open-loop data. For most manoeuvres, the driver control inputs were repeated and mean values and 95 per cent con dence intervals were computed. The simulation is driven using mean values.
Test data from the following manoeuvres are compared with simulation predictions: slowly increasing steer, step steer, pulse steer, double lane change, straight line braking and straight line acceleration. Developing simulation models which result in analytical predictions that compare well with experimental measurements is not an easy task, particularly when extreme manoeuvring conditions are considered. For limit performance manoeuvres, only correct trends or relatively close results should be expected, since the mechanics of these extreme conditions (especially tyres) are extremely diYcult to model. For NADS, the vehicle simulations are limited to near loss of control conditions, and the simulation is stopped if severe yaw rates (spin-outs) or high roll angles (imminent tip-ups) are predicted.

