3.2.3 Lane change manoeuvres
Besides evaluation of steady state, transient and frequency response predictions, NADSdyna is checked against manoeuvres that replicate real-world complex scenarios such as lane-change manoeuvres. No statistical analysis was done since only one run was performed for each manoeuvre. The steering input represents driver– vehicle closed-loop performance, with suYcient amplitude to produce moderate to high lateral accelerations. Figure 9 shows that NADSdyna does a good job of predicting timing and peaks of vehicle responses.
3.2.4 Pulse steer frequency response
Pulse steer manoeuvres are used to generate vehicle frequency responses to steering inputs. By selecting a short duration of pulse input, it is possible to excite a full range of frequencies using a single experimental test or simulation run. In eld experiments, a pulse with duration of about 0.2 s is generated which produces good steering power up to 3–4 Hz [25].
NADSdyna frequency response predictions were in

generally good agreement with experimental results, particularly below 10 rad/s. Above about 10 rad/s, NADSdyna does not fully capture all of the higher-order dynamics of the actual vehicle. Nonetheless, the predictions are believed to be acceptable for NADS. ( The companion paper [19] contains frequency response graphs comparing VDANL simulation with experimental results for the Cherokee.)
3.3 Longitudinal dynamics
3.3.1 Straight-line braking
This manoeuvre is performed from mild to severe conditions. Figure 10 contains results from severe braking runs. The results show that NADSdyna predicted reasonable longitudinal deceleration and longitudinal speed consistent with the deceleration. The brake line pressures were also well predicted by the brake model.
An algorithm was added to the NADSdyna wheel spin dynamics to model kinetic energy and damping considerations at the end of severe braking. This logic provides a smooth transition from a state of high longitudinal tyre forces down to a state of relatively low forces required once the vehicle comes to a complete stop.
3.3.2 Straight-line acceleration
This manoeuvre uses a step throttle input starting from zero vehicle speed. The results show that acceleration, wheel spin rates and vehicle speed are well predicted. The trends in engine speed are similar. EVorts to tune modelled transmission shift points are currently underway. Preliminary testing on NADS has indicated that the current powertrain models provide an acceptable degree of delity for use with NADS.

